WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.760
Narwhals are easily recognized by their long tusk,

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:10.260
but this isolated species of toothed whale has been hard to research.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.820
We know a little about them, but their physiology
and metabolism

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.340
are still a puzzle to marine biologists

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:23.800
Using a specially engineered device that resembles an undersea “fitness tracker,”

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.480
researchers received some seemingly paradoxical data

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.280
in wild narwhal vital signs.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.460
In most mammals, the flight response they undergo in reaction to danger

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.080
causes the heartrate to increase,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.120
in order to help blood flow to the muscles,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.600
and supply the oxygen needed during a speedy retreat.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:49.520
For land mammals, oxygen is plentiful in the environment,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.790
But because aquatic mammals can’t breathe
water, they have to hold their breath.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:01:00.329
To help conserve this stored oxygen, they
also lower their heart rate.

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:03.620
This decrease when diving is not unusual.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.180
Narwhals usually bottom out around 10 beats per minute,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.480
but the researchers saw that as they swam away,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.580
their heart rate went much lower,

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.720
to under 4 beats per minute.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.820
The problem is that in these flight situations,
narwhals get the conflicting signal

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:24.740
to both dive quickly and beat their hearts slowly,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:29.360
As a result, their oxygen supply gets used
up very fast,

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.080
sharply decreasing the amount of time they can dive.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.300
In lab animals subjected to this same paradoxical
physiology, sudden death can occur.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:46.660
Researchers aren’t sure how these extended
periods of hypoxia affect the narwhals health,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:50.320
and with the increased incursion into their
environment by humans,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.060
these periods may become more frequent.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.920
For now, biologists will have to figure out

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:00.960
how narwhal bodies deal with this type of stress,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.500
and if anything can be done to prevent more of it.

